1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,560
A combustion question hands you three numbers that

2
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:06,400
look alike: the water's mass, its temperature rise,

3
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:08,000
and the mass of fuel burnt.

4
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:10,720
Each one has a single job.

5
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:13,280
The water's mass and its rise go into

6
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:13,840
q.

7
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:16,160
The fuel's mass gives n.

8
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:18,480
Swap them, and the answer can be out

9
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:20,720
by a factor of a hundred or more.

10
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:24,480
Here are three HSC-style questions, and the last

11
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:26,800
one hides a result that doesn't belong.

12
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:29,760
Pause and try each one, then we'll work

13
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:31,600
through it, one step at a time.

14
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:34,480
Question one.

15
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:37,280
A methanol spirit burner heats a hundred and

16
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:39,680
fifty grams of water from twenty two point

17
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:42,400
four to forty two point four degrees.

18
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:45,120
The burner's masses before and after are on

19
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:45,760
the card.

20
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:49,680
Calculate the molar enthalpy of combustion of methanol,

21
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:51,920
then the percentage of the methanol's heat that

22
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:53,120
reached the water.

23
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:54,720
Pause here to answer.

24
00:01:05,934 --> 00:01:07,053
Start with the water.

25
00:01:07,534 --> 00:01:10,733
Delta T is twenty point zero degrees, so

26
00:01:11,214 --> 00:01:13,533
q is a hundred and fifty, times four

27
00:01:13,614 --> 00:01:16,893
point one eight, times twenty: twelve thousand five

28
00:01:16,974 --> 00:01:20,173
hundred and forty joules, or twelve point five

29
00:01:20,254 --> 00:01:21,853
four zero kilojoules.

30
00:01:22,094 --> 00:01:23,533
Keep every digit for now.

31
00:01:24,014 --> 00:01:25,013
Now the fuel.

32
00:01:25,214 --> 00:01:28,133
The burner lost one point one zero grams.

33
00:01:28,334 --> 00:01:30,893
Divide by thirty two point zero four, and

34
00:01:30,974 --> 00:01:33,613
that's zero point zero three four three three

35
00:01:33,854 --> 00:01:35,133
moles of methanol.

36
00:01:37,134 --> 00:01:40,653
Delta H is minus q over n: minus

37
00:01:40,814 --> 00:01:44,013
twelve point five four zero kilojoules, over zero

38
00:01:44,174 --> 00:01:46,973
point zero three four three three moles.

39
00:01:47,214 --> 00:01:50,413
That's minus three sixty five point two six,

40
00:01:50,494 --> 00:01:53,533
so minus three hundred and sixty five kilojoules

41
00:01:53,694 --> 00:01:54,653
per mole.

42
00:01:54,734 --> 00:01:57,373
Three significant figures, from the mass lost and

43
00:01:57,414 --> 00:01:58,493
the temperature rise.

44
00:01:58,894 --> 00:02:01,933
And it's negative, because burning releases heat.

45
00:02:03,934 --> 00:02:06,253
Part b asks how much of the heat

46
00:02:06,334 --> 00:02:07,213
reached the water.

47
00:02:07,854 --> 00:02:10,253
If those zero point zero three four three

48
00:02:10,334 --> 00:02:13,693
three moles burned completely, they'd release that times

49
00:02:13,774 --> 00:02:16,653
seven hundred and twenty six: twenty four point

50
00:02:16,734 --> 00:02:18,413
nine three kilojoules.

51
00:02:18,734 --> 00:02:21,533
The water took in twelve point five four.

52
00:02:21,614 --> 00:02:24,813
That's fifty point three percent: about half, which

53
00:02:24,894 --> 00:02:27,533
is typical for a spirit burner and can.

54
00:02:27,574 --> 00:02:29,853
The rest warmed the can and the air.

55
00:02:31,867 --> 00:02:34,506
The classic slip is dividing by the grams

56
00:02:34,587 --> 00:02:36,066
instead of the moles.

57
00:02:36,347 --> 00:02:38,906
Twelve point five four over one point one

58
00:02:39,067 --> 00:02:42,346
zero gives eleven point four, but that's kilojoules

59
00:02:42,427 --> 00:02:44,506
per gram, not per mole.

60
00:02:46,534 --> 00:02:48,453
Question two runs the other way.

61
00:02:49,014 --> 00:02:50,933
A student wants to warm two hundred and

62
00:02:51,014 --> 00:02:53,733
fifty grams of water by fifteen degrees with

63
00:02:53,814 --> 00:02:54,773
an ethanol burner.

64
00:02:55,414 --> 00:02:57,973
Earlier trials showed the water takes in forty

65
00:02:58,054 --> 00:02:59,813
five percent of the heat released.

66
00:03:00,374 --> 00:03:03,093
Using the data value for ethanol, calculate the

67
00:03:03,174 --> 00:03:05,613
mass of ethanol she should expect to burn.

68
00:03:05,894 --> 00:03:07,253
Pause here to answer.

69
00:03:18,467 --> 00:03:21,506
First, the heat the water needs: two hundred

70
00:03:21,587 --> 00:03:24,386
and fifty, times four point one eight, times

71
00:03:24,427 --> 00:03:26,146
fifteen point zero.

72
00:03:26,227 --> 00:03:29,306
That's fifteen point six seven five kilojoules.

73
00:03:29,587 --> 00:03:31,586
But only forty five percent of the burner's

74
00:03:31,667 --> 00:03:34,386
heat reaches the water, so it must release

75
00:03:34,467 --> 00:03:37,266
more than that: fifteen point six seven five

76
00:03:37,507 --> 00:03:40,386
divided by zero point four five zero is

77
00:03:40,467 --> 00:03:42,946
thirty four point eight three kilojoules.

78
00:03:44,967 --> 00:03:46,086
Now the fuel.

79
00:03:46,327 --> 00:03:49,126
Each mole of ethanol releases thirteen hundred and

80
00:03:49,207 --> 00:03:52,806
sixty seven kilojoules, so thirty four point eight

81
00:03:52,927 --> 00:03:56,006
three over thirteen sixty seven is zero point

82
00:03:56,127 --> 00:03:58,646
zero two five four eight moles.

83
00:03:58,727 --> 00:04:01,686
Times forty six point zero seven: one point

84
00:04:01,767 --> 00:04:03,286
one seven grams.

85
00:04:03,367 --> 00:04:05,846
Three significant figures, from the fifteen point zero

86
00:04:05,927 --> 00:04:08,726
degrees and the forty five point zero percent.

87
00:04:10,734 --> 00:04:13,853
The classic slip is multiplying by zero point

88
00:04:13,934 --> 00:04:16,253
four five zero instead of dividing.

89
00:04:16,814 --> 00:04:19,773
That gives zero point two three eight grams.

90
00:04:20,014 --> 00:04:22,013
But if heat is lost, you need more

91
00:04:22,174 --> 00:04:24,733
fuel, not less, so the answer has to

92
00:04:24,854 --> 00:04:27,533
be bigger than the no-loss mass, zero point

93
00:04:27,614 --> 00:04:29,053
five two eight grams.

94
00:04:31,067 --> 00:04:32,026
Question three.

95
00:04:32,507 --> 00:04:36,266
A student burns propan-one-ol in four trials, heating

96
00:04:36,347 --> 00:04:38,266
two hundred and fifty grams of water each

97
00:04:38,347 --> 00:04:38,826
time.

98
00:04:39,387 --> 00:04:42,106
The table gives each temperature rise, the mass

99
00:04:42,147 --> 00:04:44,426
the burner lost, and three of the molar

100
00:04:44,507 --> 00:04:45,226
enthalpies.

101
00:04:45,867 --> 00:04:48,426
Calculate the molar enthalpy for trial three.

102
00:04:48,907 --> 00:04:51,706
Then explain whether trial three should be included

103
00:04:51,787 --> 00:04:53,866
in the mean, and calculate the mean.

104
00:04:54,427 --> 00:04:55,866
Pause here to answer.

105
00:05:07,067 --> 00:05:09,346
Trial three, same steps.

106
00:05:09,627 --> 00:05:12,106
q is two hundred and fifty, times four

107
00:05:12,267 --> 00:05:15,706
point one eight, times eighteen point two: nineteen

108
00:05:15,787 --> 00:05:17,786
thousand and nineteen joules.

109
00:05:18,427 --> 00:05:20,746
n is one point six two over sixty

110
00:05:20,907 --> 00:05:24,266
point zero nine: zero point zero two six

111
00:05:24,427 --> 00:05:25,546
nine six moles.

112
00:05:26,107 --> 00:05:28,586
So delta H is minus seven hundred and

113
00:05:28,707 --> 00:05:30,466
five kilojoules per mole.

114
00:05:30,747 --> 00:05:32,746
The other three trials all sit close to

115
00:05:32,827 --> 00:05:35,466
minus nine seventy five, so this one is

116
00:05:35,627 --> 00:05:36,826
far less negative.

117
00:05:38,834 --> 00:05:40,113
So does it belong?

118
00:05:40,594 --> 00:05:41,633
Look for the cause.

119
00:05:42,194 --> 00:05:45,073
Trial three's temperature rise matches the others, but

120
00:05:45,154 --> 00:05:47,393
the burner lost nearly half a gram more.

121
00:05:48,034 --> 00:05:50,593
Extra mass with no extra heat means that

122
00:05:50,674 --> 00:05:52,353
fuel never heated the water.

123
00:05:52,914 --> 00:05:55,553
Most likely it left without burning: the burner

124
00:05:55,634 --> 00:05:58,833
was left uncapped and evaporated, or some spilled.

125
00:05:59,314 --> 00:06:01,873
That makes n too big, and delta H

126
00:06:01,954 --> 00:06:02,833
less negative.

127
00:06:03,314 --> 00:06:05,793
It's an anomaly with a likely cause, so

128
00:06:05,874 --> 00:06:07,553
leave it out of the mean, and repeat

129
00:06:07,634 --> 00:06:08,833
that trial if you can.

130
00:06:10,834 --> 00:06:12,313
Average the other three.

131
00:06:12,594 --> 00:06:15,553
Minus nine seventy four, minus nine seventy seven

132
00:06:15,794 --> 00:06:18,433
and minus nine seventy one give a mean

133
00:06:18,514 --> 00:06:21,233
of minus nine hundred and seventy four kilojoules

134
00:06:21,314 --> 00:06:22,153
per mole.

135
00:06:22,434 --> 00:06:24,273
That's about forty eight percent of the data

136
00:06:24,354 --> 00:06:27,393
value, in line with question one's fifty, so

137
00:06:27,474 --> 00:06:30,273
the three that fit look like normal trials.

138
00:06:30,514 --> 00:06:32,513
Only drop a result when you can explain

139
00:06:32,594 --> 00:06:32,913
it.

140
00:06:34,934 --> 00:06:38,133
The classic mistake is averaging all four.

141
00:06:38,294 --> 00:06:40,773
Trial three drags the mean to minus nine

142
00:06:40,854 --> 00:06:43,333
hundred and seven, a value that no good

143
00:06:43,494 --> 00:06:44,293
trial gave.

144
00:06:46,300 --> 00:06:49,820
So for any combustion calculation: the water's mass

145
00:06:49,900 --> 00:06:52,620
and rise go into q, the fuel's mass

146
00:06:52,780 --> 00:06:55,980
lost gives n, and delta H is minus

147
00:06:56,140 --> 00:06:59,260
q over n, in kilojoules per mole.

148
00:06:59,340 --> 00:07:01,100
If only part of the heat reaches the

149
00:07:01,260 --> 00:07:03,620
water, divide by that fraction to find the

150
00:07:03,660 --> 00:07:04,940
heat released.

151
00:07:05,020 --> 00:07:07,420
And when one trial doesn't fit, find its

152
00:07:07,620 --> 00:07:09,500
cause before you leave it out.

153
00:07:09,580 --> 00:07:12,780
Next, where an alcohol goes when it reacts.

154
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:16,240
Here are the key notes.

155
